WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.816
Tooling helps find integer defects
earlier, but it does not replace safe design.

2
00:00:04.896 --> 00:00:07.879
A warning, static analysis report,
sanitizer finding,

3
00:00:07.959 --> 00:00:12.149
or boundary-test failure is evidence that
the numeric contract needs review.

4
00:00:12.229 --> 00:00:16.481
Treat findings as engineering feedback
rather than as isolated cleanup tasks.

5
00:00:16.561 --> 00:00:20.053
Compiler warnings can identify signed and
unsigned mismatches,

6
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:24.004
narrowing conversions, constant overflows,
suspicious comparisons,

7
00:00:24.084 --> 00:00:28.528
ignored return values, and expressions
that depend on questionable assumptions.

8
00:00:28.608 --> 00:00:33.051
Warning settings should be consistent
enough that new warnings are visible during

9
00:00:33.131 --> 00:00:35.352
code review and continuous integration.

10
00:00:35.432 --> 00:00:39.558
Static analysis can find allocation-size
overflows, conversion mistakes,

11
00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:42.558
unchecked arithmetic, unsafe shifts, risky
indexes,

12
00:00:42.638 --> 00:00:46.700
and numeric values that move from input
into memory-sensitive operations.

13
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.160
Static tools are especially useful when
numeric data crosses multiple functions

14
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.588
before it controls allocation or indexing.

15
00:00:53.668 --> 00:00:58.239
Sanitizers can detect some undefined
behavior and arithmetic issues in development

16
00:00:58.319 --> 00:00:59.207
and test builds.

17
00:00:59.287 --> 00:01:02.778
They are most useful when tests cover
uncommon values: zero, one,

18
00:01:02.858 --> 00:01:06.921
negative values, maximum values,
near-maximum values, too-large values,

19
00:01:07.001 --> 00:01:10.936
boundary lengths, malformed numeric input,
and conversion boundaries.

20
00:01:11.016 --> 00:01:13.491
Defensive fuzzing may be used to find
crashes,

21
00:01:13.571 --> 00:01:17.570
parser inconsistencies, rejected-input
gaps, and unexpected behavior.

22
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.364
The goal is robust engineering:

23
00:01:19.444 --> 00:01:22.745
identify inputs that the program should
handle predictably,

24
00:01:22.825 --> 00:01:25.046
then improve validation and error paths.

25
00:01:25.126 --> 00:01:28.871
Findings should become regression tests
and review checklist items.

26
00:01:28.951 --> 00:01:32.823
Code review should trace numeric values
from input through validation,

27
00:01:32.903 --> 00:01:37.664
conversion, arithmetic, allocation,
indexing, copying, logging, and error handling.

28
00:01:37.744 --> 00:01:41.552
Reviewers should ask which type each value
has, which range is allowed,

29
00:01:41.632 --> 00:01:43.219
where that range is enforced,

30
00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:46.473
and whether the result is still valid
after each operation.

31
00:01:46.553 --> 00:01:51.313
Production diagnostics should record safe
failure context without exposing sensitive

32
00:01:51.393 --> 00:01:52.980
content or raw internal data.
